---
title: Data Flow
description: How data moves through the Lament system across ingestion, API requests, and HLS audio streaming.
section: Architecture
order: 3
---

## Ingestion Flow

The scraper pipeline processes music catalog data in this order:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Metadata["1. Search Spotify / iTunes for Metadata"] --> Match["2. Match YouTube Audio Source"]
    Match --> Extract["3. Direct yt-dlp Audio Extraction (Opus/AAC)"]
    Extract --> HLS["4. FFmpeg HLS Packaging (.m3u8 + .ts)"]
    HLS --> Visual["5. Fetch Last.fm / Deezer Visual Assets"]
    Visual --> Sharp["6. Process WebP Image Variants via sharp"]
    Sharp --> Lyrics["7. Fetch Synced LRC & Plain Lyrics"]
    Lyrics --> R2["8. Upload HLS Audio & WebP Artwork to R2"]
    R2 --> LibSQL["9. Insert Track Record into LibSQL Database"]
```

---

## Request Flow

When a listener interacts with the Lament web client:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Browser["Client Browser"] -->|"Sends Request"| Proxy["SvelteKit Server Route (+server.ts)"]
    Proxy -->|"Injects x-api-key / X-Master-Key"| API["Hono Edge API (Cloudflare Workers)"]
    API --> LibSQL["Turso LibSQL (Data)"]
    API --> LRU["In-Memory Cache (LRU)"]
    API --> R2["Cloudflare R2 (Files)"]
```

Key points:

- The browser **never** communicates directly with the closed `lament-api` backend.
- Dual-layer credentials (`x-api-key` / `X-Master-Key`) are injected server-side by SvelteKit proxy routes.
- Cached endpoints utilize HTTP ETag validation (`304 Not Modified`).

---

## Audio Streaming Flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Player["Browser HLS.js"] -->|1. GET /api/stream/track_id/playlist.m3u8| Proxy["SvelteKit Server Proxy"]
    Proxy -->|2. Forward Request| API["Hono Edge API"]
    API -->|3. Fetch Manifest| R2["Cloudflare R2 Storage"]
    R2 -->|4. Return Manifest| Player
    Player -->|5. Fetch .ts Segments| CDN["Cloudflare Edge CDN"]
```

`HLS.js` handles segment fetching, audio buffer allocation, and gapless playback.
